{"title": "Ask HN: What are you doing to protect the privacy of your notes?", "link": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50349024", "summary": "<p>I’ve used many note-taking tools. But over time, my expectations have changed. Right now, my focus is entirely on privacy. When I looked at privacy-focused note-taking tools, I couldn’t make up my mind.<p>For example, I’m currently using Obsidian. But some of its features aren’t quite what I want, and it still feels like it’s lacking in terms of privacy.<p>I wanted to ask this question in case you use any privacy-focused note-taking tools or have some techniques you use with existing tools.<p>Such as, I came up with an idea like this for Obsidian, but I didn’t implement it. It keeps notes in a folder. I even thought about creating something that would constantly scan that folder using Python so I could encrypt new notes and store them on my own server. But as I said, I didn’t do it. It was just an idea.<p>I haven’t done it yet. I just wanted to ask you all to expand my knowledge.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Comments URL: <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50349024\">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50349024</a></p>\n<p>Points: 2</p>\n<p># Comments: 2</p>", "published": "2026-11-03T07:15:25+00:00", "source": "Hacker News Best", "category": "techblog", "matched_entities": {}, "ontology": {"repo": "BlogRadar", "category": "techblog", "ontology_version": "0.1.0", "source_event_model": "blog_post", "event_model_id": "tech.blog_post", "evidence_policy_ids": ["source_metadata", "article_text"], "event_model_payload": {"headline": "Ask HN: What are you doing to protect the privacy of your notes?", "source_name": "Hacker News Best", "source_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50349024", "published_at": "2026-11-03T07:15:25+00:00", "summary": "<p>I’ve used many note-taking tools. But over time, my expectations have changed. Right now, my focus is entirely on privacy. When I looked at privacy-focused note-taking tools, I couldn’t make up my mind.<p>For example, I’m currently using Obsidian. But some of its features aren’t quite what I want, and it still feels like it’s lacking in terms of privacy.<p>I wanted to ask this question in case you use any privacy-focused note-taking tools or have some techniques you use with existing tools.<p>Such as, I came up with an idea like this for Obsidian, but I didn’t implement it. It keeps notes in a folder. I even thought about creating something that would constantly scan that folder using Python so I could encrypt new notes and store them on my own server. But as I said, I didn’t do it. It was just an idea.<p>I haven’t done it yet. I just wanted to ask you all to expand my knowledge.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Comments URL: <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50349024\">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50349024</a></p>\n<p>Points: 2</p>\n<p># Comments: 2</p>"}}, "logged_at": "2026-11-03T07:37:23.632757+00:00"}
{"title": "Show HN: OpenYog, SQLyog Community Ported to Qt 6 for Linux and Windows", "link": "https://github.com/AbdulOhab/openyog", "summary": "<p>Webyog stopped SQLyog Community version, so I built OpenYog by porting it to Qt 6. It's a desktop database client for MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQLite and MongoDB on Linux and Windows.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Comments URL: <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50349003\">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50349003</a></p>\n<p>Points: 2</p>\n<p># Comments: 0</p>", "published": "2026-11-03T07:12:04+00:00", "source": "Hacker News Best", "category": "techblog", "matched_entities": {}, "ontology": {"repo": "BlogRadar", "category": "techblog", "ontology_version": "0.1.0", "source_event_model": "blog_post", "event_model_id": "tech.blog_post", "evidence_policy_ids": ["source_metadata", "article_text"], "event_model_payload": {"headline": "Show HN: OpenYog, SQLyog Community Ported to Qt 6 for Linux and Windows", "source_name": "Hacker News Best", "source_url": "https://github.com/AbdulOhab/openyog", "published_at": "2026-11-03T07:12:04+00:00", "summary": "<p>Webyog stopped SQLyog Community version, so I built OpenYog by porting it to Qt 6. It's a desktop database client for MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQLite and MongoDB on Linux and Windows.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Comments URL: <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50349003\">https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50349003</a></p>\n<p>Points: 2</p>\n<p># Comments: 0</p>"}}, "logged_at": "2026-11-03T07:37:23.632757+00:00"}
